content.text:
Found it. This commit `1bfc9d0cc2` introduced `failedRemoveCinderDisks` as `null` and then, in the catch block, called `failedRemoveCinderDisks.addAll(cinderDisks)` on the still-null variable — an NPE waiting to happen, which was fixed later by `ae33192d31`.

## Bug-Introducing Commits
BIC: 1bfc9d0cc2e5e3a61fe3985d74b44efc6d6366a4

## Reasoning

**(1) Investigation:** The fix commit `ae33192d31` changes a single line in `RemoveVmCommand.java`, inside the catch block of `removeCinderDisks()`, replacing `failedRemoveCinderDisks.addAll(cinderDisks);` with `failedRemoveCinderDisks = cinderDisks;`. I ran `git log -p` on that file to find when this method/line was first introduced.

**(2) Specific change:** Commit `1bfc9d0cc2` ("core: Support remove of VM with Cinder disks") added the entire `removeCinderDisks()` method. In that method:
```java
Collection<CinderDisk> failedRemoveCinderDisks = null;
...
try {
    failedRemoveCinderDisks = future.get().getActionReturnValue();
} catch (InterruptedException | ExecutionException e) {
    failedRemoveCinderDisks.addAll(cinderDisks);   // <-- bug: NPE, variable is still null here
    log.error("Exception", e);
}
```
The variable `failedRemoveCinderDisks` is initialized to `null` and is only assigned inside the `try` block. If an `InterruptedException` or `ExecutionException` is thrown, the assignment in the `try` never happens, so in the `catch` block the variable is still `null`, and calling `.addAll(...)` on it throws a `NullPointerException`.

**(3) Evidence:** The fix diff exactly reverses this defect by assigning the collection directly (`failedRemoveCinderDisks = cinderDisks;`) instead of calling `addAll` on a null reference. Tracing the file history confirms this exact line, with the exact same null-initialization pattern, was introduced verbatim by `1bfc9d0cc2` and was never modified in between (no intervening commits touch this method per `git log --oneline` on the file). I'm highly confident this is the bug-introducing commit.

